problem link: https://leetcode.com/problems/construct-binary-tree-from-inorder-and-postorder-traversal/description/
Intuition
The postorder traversal follows:

Left → Right → Root

Therefore, the last element of postorder is always the root of the current subtree.

The inorder traversal tells us how the tree splits:

Left subtree | Root | Right subtree

Since we process postorder from the end:

Root → Right → Left

the right subtree must be constructed before the left subtree.

A hashmap is used to quickly find the root's position in inorder.

Approach
Create a hashmap storing each value's index in inorder.
Start a pointer idx at the last index of postorder.
Recursively construct the tree:
The current root is postorder[idx].
Decrement idx.
Find the root's position in inorder.
Build the right subtree first because postorder is being processed backwards.
Build the left subtree.
Return the root.

Complexity Analysis
Time Complexity: O(n)

Every node is processed exactly once.
Hashmap lookup is O(1).

Space Complexity: O(n)

Hashmap requires O(n) space.
Recursion depth can be O(n) for a skewed tree.

Code
# Definition for a binary tree node.
# class TreeNode:
#     def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):
#         self.val = val
#         self.left = left
#         self.right = right
class Solution:
    def buildTree(self, inorder: List[int], postorder: List[int]) -> Optional[TreeNode]:
        inorder_map = {}
        for i,val in enumerate(inorder):
            inorder_map[val] = i
        idx = len(postorder) - 1
        def fun(low,high):
            nonlocal idx
            if low > high:
                return None
            root = TreeNode(postorder[idx])
            mid = inorder_map[postorder[idx]]
            idx -= 1
            root.right = fun(mid+1,high)
            root.left = fun(low,mid-1)
            return root
        return fun(0,len(inorder)-1)
